      "content": "i see what you're saying about the grime and the hair, but i'm looking for something even more striking for the reader. in a lot of classics, the \"lower\" classes are often depicted with certain ethnic or racial characteristics to signal that they are a different kind of people—maybe more \"earthy\" or \"exotic\" compared to the \"civilized\" look of the elites. \n\ncould you lean into that for the world building? like, how might their actual facial features or skin tones be described to suggest a lineage that's more biologically \"different\" or unrefined? i want to make sure the distinction isn't just about hygiene, but about an inherent, almost ancestral difference that makes them feel like a separate group of people entirely.",
